  "evidence": "TEXT_D: £2bn/year for education with smaller primary classes and universal early years places; concrete NHS boosts (at least £540m annually, maximum six‑month waits, restore free eye/dental checks); 3,000 more police and stronger victim support; investment in public transport while resisting further rail/London Underground privatisation; build more affordable homes and end rough sleeping. TEXT_F: Emphasis on tax cuts (aiming for 20p basic rate), completing and extending privatisations (rail, London Underground, NATS, utilities competition), expanding school selection/Assisted Places, and tougher sentencing/CCTV with limited new funding for core public services.",
  "reasoning": "In 1997 voters prioritised improving public services—especially education, the NHS, and crime reduction—over further privatisation and large tax cuts, and TEXT_D concentrates resources and reforms directly on those areas. TEXT_F focuses more on tax, privatisation, and selection, which were less aligned with the electorate’s top concerns that year.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
